WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.359 --> 00:00:04.540
When The Shield premiered on FX in 2002, the
program pushed the limits of what could be

00:00:04.540 --> 00:00:06.530
portrayed on cable programming.

00:00:06.530 --> 00:00:10.571
It's been well over a decade since the series
helped spark a small-screen revolution, so

00:00:10.571 --> 00:00:14.370
let's look back at some little-known facts
about this extraordinary show.

00:00:14.370 --> 00:00:17.450
This is the untold truth of The Shield.

00:00:17.450 --> 00:00:18.610
Risky business

00:00:18.610 --> 00:00:23.950
Before 2002, the only original series on FX
was the Baywatch parody Son of the Beach.

00:00:23.950 --> 00:00:28.370
Once The Shield joined the lineup, the series
broke new ground, testing the limits on violence,

00:00:28.370 --> 00:00:29.970
nudity, and language.

00:00:29.970 --> 00:00:31.740
It was very risky material.

00:00:31.740 --> 00:00:36.440
"Open your mouth - drop the knife.

00:00:36.650 --> 00:00:37.800
SPIT IT!"

00:00:37.810 --> 00:00:42.400
The Emmy-winning series centered around corrupt
cop Vic Mackey, masterfully played by Michael

00:00:42.400 --> 00:00:43.400
Chiklis.

00:00:43.400 --> 00:00:47.950
Mackey leads an experimental anti-gang unit
within the L.A.P.D., often using highly unethical

00:00:47.950 --> 00:00:49.700
means to fight crime.

00:00:49.700 --> 00:00:53.450
Inspired by the success of The Shield, the
network brought other edgy new shows into

00:00:53.450 --> 00:00:56.420
the fold like Nip/Tuck, Damages, and Rescue
Me.

00:00:56.420 --> 00:00:57.420
Chiklis told TV Week:

00:00:57.420 --> 00:01:00.100
"I think the face of basic cable was changed
forever.

00:01:00.100 --> 00:01:02.750
It made a lot of people think, 'Why can't
we?'"

00:01:02.750 --> 00:01:05.030
The Walking Dead connection

00:01:05.030 --> 00:01:08.840
In 2007, Frank Darabont, the writer-director
of The Shawshank Redemption, took a break

00:01:08.840 --> 00:01:11.860
from big-budget filmmaking to direct an episode
of The Shield.

00:01:11.860 --> 00:01:16.380
After filming the season six episode "Chasing
Ghosts," Daramont wanted to further explore

00:01:16.380 --> 00:01:17.619
television's possibilities.

00:01:17.619 --> 00:01:21.620
It wasn't long before he started developing
a zombie horror series based on the comic

00:01:21.620 --> 00:01:22.950
book The Walking Dead.

00:01:22.950 --> 00:01:26.940
Bringing The Walking Dead to TV proved to
be an uphill battle with network execs, but

00:01:26.940 --> 00:01:31.590
when AMC finally greenlit the post-apocalyptic
series, Darabont remembered his roots and

00:01:31.590 --> 00:01:36.100
enlisted some of The Shield's production team,
including Charles Eglee and Glen Mazzara,

00:01:36.100 --> 00:01:38.270
the Walking Dead's future showrunner.

00:01:38.270 --> 00:01:40.549
Michael Chiklis' transformation

00:01:40.549 --> 00:01:44.260
Before The Shield, Michael Chiklis was known
for playing much cuddlier characters, like

00:01:44.260 --> 00:01:47.580
Tony Scali, the police officer in ABC's The
Commish.

00:01:47.580 --> 00:01:51.420
But the actor was tired of being typecast,
telling the Archive of American Television

00:01:51.420 --> 00:01:56.259
that a conversation with his wife Michelle
Morán inspired him to make a career change.

00:01:56.259 --> 00:01:57.259
She told him:

00:01:57.259 --> 00:02:04.119
"It's not incumbent upon the studios and um
the networks to reinvent you.

00:02:04.119 --> 00:02:06.370
It's incumbent upon yourself to invent yourself.

00:02:06.370 --> 00:02:10.410
That reinvention, of course, required Chiklis
to also reinvent his physique in order to

00:02:10.410 --> 00:02:11.980
land different kinds of roles.

00:02:11.980 --> 00:02:15.950
According to Men's Journal, Chiklis relied
on "healthy, portion-controlled meals" and

00:02:15.950 --> 00:02:19.730
ran seven miles a day, six days a week to
transform his appearance.

00:02:19.730 --> 00:02:23.610
"I just hit the gym three hours a day.

00:02:23.610 --> 00:02:25.780
And I got in sick shape at that time."

00:02:25.780 --> 00:02:29.260
The hard work paid off, despite what Family
Guy would lead you to believe:

00:02:29.260 --> 00:02:34.110
"No matter how muscular I get, I will never
be muscular."

00:02:34.110 --> 00:02:35.980
Walton Goggins' ordeal

00:02:35.980 --> 00:02:40.310
Before The Shield took TV by storm, Walton
Goggins was known mainly for bit parts in

00:02:40.310 --> 00:02:42.300
'90s programs like In the Heat of the Night.

00:02:43.010 --> 00:02:45.020
"You bring it or not?

00:02:45.020 --> 00:02:47.400
"I've always got it, man."

00:02:47.970 --> 00:02:51.300
Taking on the role of Shane Vendrell put the
future star in the spotlight.

00:02:51.319 --> 00:02:53.590
But execs hated his performance early on.

00:02:53.590 --> 00:02:55.650
In fact, he was almost fired from the show.

00:02:55.650 --> 00:02:57.140
He told Nashville Scene:

00:02:57.140 --> 00:03:00.450
"The brass at FX saw the pilot, and they thought
[…] is he irritating?

00:03:00.450 --> 00:03:01.800
[…] I can't read him.'"

00:03:01.800 --> 00:03:05.550
If Goggins had never played Shane in The Shield,
he may have never gotten to play memorable

00:03:05.550 --> 00:03:09.790
characters like Justified's Boyd Crowder or
The Hateful Eight's Chris Mannix.

00:03:09.790 --> 00:03:12.330
Would anyone want to live in that reality?

00:03:12.330 --> 00:03:14.410
"I wouldn't.

00:03:14.410 --> 00:03:16.489
I wouldn't""

00:03:16.489 --> 00:03:17.519
Game over

00:03:17.519 --> 00:03:22.580
Back in 2006, Fox teamed up with Sony to release
24: The Game for the PlayStation 2.

00:03:22.580 --> 00:03:26.770
Kiefer Sutherland fans got to step into Jack
Bauer's shoes, battling bad guys against a

00:03:26.770 --> 00:03:27.770
ticking clock.

00:03:27.770 --> 00:03:31.550
A year later, The Shield tried the same formula:
a game based on the show was released for

00:03:31.550 --> 00:03:33.459
the PlayStation 2 and PC.

00:03:33.459 --> 00:03:36.659
Much like the 24 game, The Shield was a third
person shooter.

00:03:36.659 --> 00:03:40.900
The game involved a gang war between two rival
crews, and it wasn't well-received at all.

00:03:40.900 --> 00:03:42.820
In its two-star review, IGN said:

00:03:42.820 --> 00:03:46.720
"You'd be better off avoiding this one, as
it makes great strides to damage the otherwise

00:03:46.720 --> 00:03:48.489
high-quality of the franchise."

00:03:48.489 --> 00:03:50.110
In other words,

00:03:50.110 --> 00:03:52.190
"This experiment's failed"

00:03:52.190 --> 00:03:53.260
"Go on…

00:03:53.260 --> 00:03:54.620
Arrest me."

00:03:54.989 --> 00:03:56.340
A murderous reality

00:03:56.349 --> 00:04:00.519
Throughout the run of the show, actor Michael
Jace played the role of Officer Julien Lowe,

00:04:00.519 --> 00:04:04.680
a strict Christian who hid his homosexuality
from his wife and crew.

00:04:04.680 --> 00:04:07.370
In 2014, the actor's career came to a violent
halt.

00:04:07.370 --> 00:04:11.629
Jace was arrested for fatally shooting his
wife April in front of their two sons.

00:04:11.629 --> 00:04:14.959
Prosecutors claimed the horrific crime was
motivated by jealousy.

00:04:14.959 --> 00:04:17.509
The Mirror shared part of the trial's transcript,
claiming:

00:04:17.509 --> 00:04:21.349
"Jace admitted he was 'angry' at the time,
and said: 'All I intended to do was shoot

00:04:21.349 --> 00:04:22.409
her in the leg.

00:04:22.409 --> 00:04:24.529
And then I shot her in the leg, and that was
it.'"

00:04:24.529 --> 00:04:29.110
Jace was convicted of second-degree murder
and sentenced to 40 years to life in prison.

00:04:29.110 --> 00:04:31.279
A Shield reunion?

00:04:31.279 --> 00:04:35.129
TV reboots like the recently revived Will
&amp; Grace are all the rage right now, so it's

00:04:35.129 --> 00:04:38.699
understandable that fans of The Shield wonder
if the show might return one day.

00:04:38.699 --> 00:04:42.559
Sounds like series creator Shawn Ryan is kicking
the idea around, too.

00:04:42.559 --> 00:04:46.029
During a panel discussion at the 2016's ATX
Television Festival, he said:

00:04:46.029 --> 00:04:52.800
"I don't know where Vic Mackey is until someone
puts me in a writers' room."

00:04:52.800 --> 00:04:54.759
So could Vic Mackey return one day?

00:04:54.759 --> 00:04:56.249
Ryan tells Entertainment Weekly:

00:04:56.249 --> 00:04:59.590
"I've never definitively decided what Vic
Mackey is doing now.

00:04:59.590 --> 00:05:01.979
As a fan of The Shield, I am interested in
seeing that."

00:05:01.979 --> 00:05:02.979
So are we.

00:05:02.979 --> 00:05:03.979
Thanks for watching!

